README.md
    1→# Tock
    5→[Features](#features) • [Quick Start](#quick-start) • [Commands](#commands) •
    6→[Configuration](#configuration) • [Theme](#theme) • [File Format](#file-format) • [Shell](#shell-completion) • [Inspiration](#inspiration) • [License](#license)
    8→## Features
    10→<img src="assets/demo.png" width="820px" />
    12→**Tock** is a powerful time tracking tool for the command line. It saves activity logs as plaintext files and provides an interactive terminal UI for viewing your time.
    14→Features include:
    16→- 📝 **Simple plaintext format** - Activities stored in human-readable files
    17→- 📅 **Notes & Tags** - Attach detailed notes and tags to activities
    18→- 🎨 **Interactive TUI** - Beautiful terminal calendar view using Bubble Tea
    19→- ⚡ **Fast & Lightweight** - Single binary, no dependencies
    20→- 🔄 **Compatible** - Reads/writes Bartib file format and TimeWarrior data files
    21→- 🎨 **Customizable Themes** - Multiple color themes and custom color support
    22→- 📅 **iCal Export** - Generate .ics files for calendar integration, or sync with system calendars (macOS only)
    24→<hr clear="right"/>
    26→## Quick Start
    28→### Installation
    30→#### Homebrew (macOS)
    32→```bash
    33→# https://formulae.brew.sh/formula/tock
    34→# Install from official repository (build from source)
    35→brew install tock
    37→# Install pre-built binary via latest release (Goreleaser)
    38→brew install kriuchkov/tap/tock
    39→```
    41→#### Go Install
    43→```bash
    44→go install github.com/kriuchkov/tock/cmd/tock@latest
    45→```
    47→#### Build from source
    49→```bash
    50→go build -o tock ./cmd/tock
    51→```
    53→#### Install Script (Linux & macOS)
    55→```bash
    56→curl -sS https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kriuchkov/tock/master/install.sh | sh
    57→```
    59→#### Download Binary
    61→Download the latest release from the [Releases](https://github.com/kriuchkov/tock/releases) page.
    63→### Basic Usage
    65→<img src="assets/demo.gif" width="820px" />
    66→<hr clear="right"/>
    68→Start tracking time:
    70→```bash
    71→tock start # Interactive mode (requires no arguments)
    72→tock start "My Project" "Implementing features" # Quick start with arguments
    73→tock start "My Project" "Implementing features" "Some notes" "tag1,tag2"  # Quick start with arguments + notes/tags
    74→tock start -d "Implementing features" -p "My Project" # Start with flags
    75→tock start -d "Implementing features" -p "My Project" -t 14:30 --note "Some notes" --tag "afternoon" # Start with flags + time + notes/tags
    76→```
    78→Stop the current activity:
    80→```bash
    81→tock stop # Stop now
    82→tock stop -t 17:00 # Stop at specific time
    83→tock stop --note "Finished for the day" # Stop with a note
    84→tock stop --tag "end-of-day" # Stop with a tag
    85→```
    87→Continue a previous activity (Create a new entry with the same project/description):
    89→```bash
    90→tock continue  # Continue the last activity
    91→tock continue 1   # Continue the 2nd to last activity
    92→tock continue -d "New" # Continue last activity but with new description
    93→tock continue -p "New Project" # Continue last activity but with new project
    94→tock continue -t 14:00 # Continue last activity but with specific start time
    95→tock continue --note "Resuming work" --tag "resumed" # Continue last activity with a note and tag
    96→```
    98→View activities in interactive calendar:
    100→```bash
    101→tock list
    102→```
    104→Calendar controls:
    106→- `Arrow Keys` / `h,j,k,l`: Navigate days
    107→- `n`: Next month
    108→- `p`: Previous month
    109→- `q` / `Esc`: Quit
    111→```bash
    112→tock calendar
    113→```
    602→## Update Check
    604→Tock automatically checks for updates once a week. When a new version is available, you will see a notification after running commands like `start` or `stop`.
    606→You can disable this check by setting `check_updates: false` in your configuration file or using the environment variable `TOCK_CHECK_UPDATES=false`.
    608→## File Format
    610→Activities are stored in plaintext format (compatible with Bartib):
    612→```
    613→  2025-12-10 09:00 - 2025-12-10 11:30 | Project Name | Task description
    614→  2025-12-10 13:00 | Another Project | Ongoing task
    615→```
    617→You can edit this file manually with any text editor.
    619→## Shell Completion
    621→Tock supports shell completion for Bash, Zsh, Fish, and PowerShell. This includes basic command completion and **intelligent suggestions** for:
    623→- **Projects**: Suggests existing project names when using `-p` / `--project`.
    624→- **Descriptions**: Suggests recent activity descriptions when using `-d` / `--description`.
    625→  - Suggestions are context-aware: if a project is selected, it only suggests descriptions previously used for that project.
    626→- **Continue IDs**: Suggests valid activity IDs (with details) for the `tock continue` command.
    628→### Installation
    630→To enable shell completion (e.g. for Oh My Zsh):
    632→1. Create a directory for the plugin:
    634→```bash
    635→mkdir -p ~/.oh-my-zsh/custom/plugins/tock
    636→```
    638→1. Generate the completion script:
    640→```bash
    641→tock completion zsh > ~/.oh-my-zsh/custom/plugins/tock/_tock
    642→```
    644→1. Add `tock` to your plugins list in `~/.zshrc`:
    646→```bash
    647→plugins=(... tock)
    648→```
    650→1. Restart your shell:
    652→```bash
    653→exec zsh
    654→```
    709→## Inspiration
    711→Tock is inspired by and compatible with [Bartib](https://github.com/nikolassv/bartib) - an excellent time tracking tool written in Rust by Nikolas Schmidt-Voigt. It's saved me countless hours and helped me stay organized, so I wanted to create a similar tool in Go with a clean architecture approach and an interactive terminal UI.
    713→## License
    715→GPL-3.0-or-later
